ios: the 3D scene drawn again only where it changed

With the camera still, little of the 3D scene changes from one frame to the next (0.3-1.1 % of its 16 px tiles at
Lumbridge and at Varrock West with 500 bots), yet every frame drew all of it again: 11-14k triangles, 85-88 % of a
frame's work. Now a frame with the camera, level, viewport and zoom of the frame before draws again only the tiles
where something it draws changed, with exactly the pixels a full render gives (dash3d/IncrRender.ts):

- Pass 1 is upstream's traversal (World.renderAll) unchanged, every side effect in its order (getTempModel and what it
  sets, minY, picking, the ground pick, getTexels: which textures animate depends on it), drawing nothing. Each draw
  item, a ground tile or a model placement, is matched with the item of the reference frame (the last one drawn) by its
  object and arguments; a source (a player, NPC, projectile, spot anim, animated loc or ground item) also by the content
  of the model getTempModel gave it (IncrSig: the vertices and face alphas by value, the face arrays shared with a
  cached model by identity), so a player between two frames of an idle animation matches. A matched item is left as it
  was: no projection, no face sorting, the reference's screen box and a copy of its recorded triangles. Any other is
  recorded triangle by triangle (a full frame records every item while it draws). Never left as it was: a model the
  pointer may pick, anything asking for texture 17 or 24, the ground while a click is pending.
- The region drawn again: the boxes of every item without a match and every reference item left without one, the items
  using texture 17 or 24 when it moved, and each tile where two matched items are drawn in another order than before.
  World.fill's tile order depends on every sprite on the tiles, so a player moving can make two unchanged locs elsewhere
  swap, and the last one drawn wins their overlap.
- Blends write each pixel from the one to its right (dst[off++] = ... dst[off]): where the region meets a blending
  item's box in a row of tiles, it covers that box to one pixel past its right edge, to a fixpoint. An unchanged blend
  left of a changed pixel is then drawn again, and no blend that is drawn reads a pixel the region does not draw.
- The region is cleared in a scratch frame and drawn again from the recorded triangles, in order, a triangle only when
  its box meets the region and a span only when it meets the region's columns in its row; that goes into the
  persistent frame, and the persistent frame to the output. On average the region is 5 % of the frame at Lumbridge
  and 9-11 % at Varrock West with 500 bots.

A frame is drawn in full, as upstream draws it, when the camera, level, viewport or zoom moved since the frame before,
and the next one measures every item's box. Also in full: after any change to the scene (World's set/del/add/move/
reset/shareLight methods, LOC_ANIM), to the colour table, the textures or the clipping, after PixMap.invalidate
(visibility, pageshow, a restored context, a resize), at least every 64 frames and 1.5 s (a safety net), and when the
region would be more than 60 % of the frame. An error inside it turns it off and draws upstream's frame from then on.
World.incrBypass draws upstream's frame without any of it (tests), World.incrLast says what the last renderAll did,
IncrRender.enabled = false turns it off.

Debug builds only (LC_INCR_VERIFY, web/client/build.mjs; release bundles leave it out): IncrRender.verify draws each
frame first as upstream does, from the state the frame starts with, puts that state back, lets the build draw the
frame, and compares every pixel and the state a frame leaves (the picked entities in order, the ground pick, whether
textures 17 and 24 animate, every drawn source's minY, height and animation state).

Verified on debug builds, in Chrome (V8) and WebKit (JavaScriptCore, a macOS WKWebView): every partial frame equal
to upstream's full render of the same frame, pixels and state, none differing.
- web/test/render-verify (an outside oracle: it wraps renderAll, keeps the build's frame and draws upstream's into a
  buffer of its own): the world session's 28 scenarios (the title to the world, Lumbridge's NPCs, camera views and
  one-unit nudges, the waterfall, animated locs, the windmill, fires, doors, ground items, woodcutting, fishing, mining,
  combat, the player's own animations, roofs and levels, blends, overhead chat, menus, the HUD's panels, zoom, the
  classic layout, a resize, 30 fps, brightness, pause and resume, a relog): Chrome 29,626 partial frames, WebKit
  29,852 (at half length), and its 12-minute idle, Chrome 37,020, WebKit 36,846; its bots session (Varrock West with
  500 bots, Edgeville's PK crowd, the Wilderness, a relog, a long idle): Chrome 35,446, WebKit 35,239; its low-detail
  session (Settings › World › Detail: Low, 16 scenarios): Chrome 11,814, WebKit 11,995; the world session as the
  Android app's page (an 800x360 world, whole-frame pushes): 23,099; and the full frames from 32 fixed cameras
  identical to main's in both engines (96 of 96).
- The patch's own oracle (IncrVerify): Varrock West with 500 bots, Chrome 3,023 partial frames, WebKit 3,012;
  Lumbridge, Chrome 3,020, WebKit 3,010; hud.mjs's play-through (Chrome), 6,889 as iOS and 6,576 as the Android page;
  e2e.mjs's camera check, 427 and 424.

Measured on release builds, main and this interleaved (A B A B A B, 20 s each after a settle, one at a time, a Mac):
standing at Varrock West with 500 bots, Chrome's page main thread 331.1 -> 204.5 ms/s (-38 %; its renderer process
with the engine's worker 440 -> 331) and WebKit's WebContent process (the page and the engine) 402.9 -> 309.5 ms/s
(-23 %); at Lumbridge without bots 323.7 -> 231.0 (-29 %) and 335.4 -> 271.9 (-19 %); at Lumbridge walking every 3 s
334.3 -> 273.9 (-18 %, Chrome). 51 logic cycles a second and 50 frames a second on both. It holds 4.7 MB (Lumbridge)
to 5-7 MB (Varrock West with 500 bots) more: two 3D frames, two triangle buffers, the item lists and the sources'
signatures.

